<h2>The allegations</h2>
<p>FIR No. 101/2026, registered on 22 May 2026 at the Economic Offences Wing, concerned crypto-investment schemes operated under names including &ldquo;Mobicryp&rdquo; and a &ldquo;Minting Platform.&rdquo; The inducement was arithmetic: returns of roughly 9.98% per month. Twenty-one investors claimed deposits exceeding Rs. 3.78 crore. The investigation attributed to the petitioner receipt of about Rs. 2 crore in cash and a further Rs. 40 lakh through associated accounts; a co-accused arrested at IGI Airport on 10 September 2026 described the two as operating together.</p>

<h2>Why protection was refused</h2>
<p>The Court applied the framework of <strong>Siddharam Satlingappa Mhetre</strong> alongside the strand of authority — including the principles drawn from <strong>P. Chidambaram</strong> and allied decisions cited in the order — that treats economic offences with particular caution at the pre-arrest stage. Three findings controlled the outcome. The material prima facie connected the petitioner to the receipt of investor funds. He had not joined the investigation despite notice — the single most damaging fact in any anticipatory bail plea. And the investigation&rsquo;s next steps were precisely the kind that courts accept may require custody: tracing the money trail, identifying beneficiaries of the funds, and confronting the petitioner with documents already collected. The conclusion was categorical: <em>&ldquo;I do not consider this to be a fit case for the grant of anticipatory bail.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>The anatomy of economic-offence bail</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Where protection is granted</strong><br>Documented cooperation with the investigating agency, a traceable and explained flow of funds, a genuinely civil commercial dispute dressed as cheating, and no risk of evidence destruction.</div>
  <div class="arrow">vs</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Where it is refused</strong><br>Unexplained receipt of public deposits, non-appearance despite notice, an untraced money trail, co-accused statements describing a joint enterprise, and documents the accused must be confronted with.</div>
</div>
<p>The decision also signals how the BUDS Act, 2019 is changing the texture of deposit-fraud prosecutions in Delhi. Where schemes solicit deposits outside the regulated channels — chit funds, NBFC deposits, collective investment schemes — the Act supplies offence definitions and attachment machinery purpose-built for the subject, and its invocation alongside Sections 420/406/120B IPC (now paralleled by Sections 318, 316 and 61 of the BNS for post-July 2024 conduct) hardens the prosecution&rsquo;s position at the bail stage.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>Respond to investigation notices in person and on record; non-appearance despite notice is treated as conduct, not strategy.</p>
  <p>Reconstruct the fund flow with bank statements and ledgers before seeking protection — an explained trail is the strongest answer to the custodial-interrogation argument.</p>
  <p>Investors on the other side of such schemes should report promptly to the EOW; early complaints improve both tracing and the prospect of restitution through attachment.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">The dismissal of anticipatory bail decides custody risk, not guilt; the allegations in the FIR remain matters of investigation and trial. Regular bail after arrest is assessed on its own footing, including the stage of the investigation and the material then collected.</div>
